---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import TriangleCalculator from '@/components/tools/math/TriangleCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="triangle-calculator">
  <TriangleCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to use the triangle solver</h2>
    <p>
      Fill in any three of the six boxes and leave the rest empty. The standard labelling applies: side
      <strong>a</strong> is opposite angle <strong>A</strong>, side <strong>b</strong> is opposite angle
      <strong>B</strong>, and side <strong>c</strong> is opposite angle <strong>C</strong>. Angles are in
      degrees. The triangle calculator finds which case you entered, picks the right law and shows the working.
    </p>
    <p>
      Sides have no units, so the answer is in the unit of your input: centimetres in, centimetres out.
    </p>

    <h2>The five solvable cases</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Case</th>
            <th scope="col">What you know</th>
            <th scope="col">Method used</th>
            <th scope="col">Solutions</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>SSS</td><td>All three sides</td><td>Law of cosines for each angle</td><td>One, if the triangle inequality holds</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SAS</td><td>Two sides and the angle between them</td><td>Law of cosines for the third side</td><td>Exactly one</td></tr>
          <tr><td>ASA</td><td>Two angles and the side between them</td><td>180° sum, then law of sines</td><td>Exactly one</td></tr>
          <tr><td>AAS</td><td>Two angles and a side not between them</td><td>180° sum, then law of sines</td><td>Exactly one</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SSA</td><td>Two sides and an angle not between them</td><td>Law of sines</td><td>Zero, one or two (the ambiguous case)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      AAA (three angles) is not on the list. Three angles fix the shape but not the size, so infinitely many
      similar triangles fit. The solver needs at least one side length.
    </p>

    <h2>The law of cosines and the law of sines</h2>
    <h3>Law of cosines</h3>
    <p>
      c² = a² + b² − 2ab·cos(C). Rearranged for an angle it becomes cos(C) = (a² + b² − c²) ÷ 2ab. With C = 90°
      the cosine term is zero and the law becomes Pythagoras, so the law of cosines is Pythagoras with a
      correction term for the angle.
    </p>
    <h3>Law of sines</h3>
    <p>
      a ÷ sin(A) = b ÷ sin(B) = c ÷ sin(C). That shared ratio also equals twice the circumradius. It is the
      quickest method when you have a matched side–angle pair, but it uses arcsine, which has two answers
      between 0° and 180°. This causes the ambiguous case below.
    </p>

    <h2>The ambiguous case (SSA)</h2>
    <p>
      Suppose you know angle A, its opposite side a, and another side b. Draw angle A, draw side b along one
      arm, then draw an arc of radius a from its far end. The arc can miss the other arm, touch it once, or
      cross it twice:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>a &lt; b·sin(A): the arc is too short. No triangle exists.</li>
      <li>a = b·sin(A): the arc just touches. One right-angled triangle.</li>
      <li>b·sin(A) &lt; a &lt; b: the arc crosses twice. Two different triangles fit, one acute and one obtuse at B.</li>
      <li>a ≥ b: only one crossing is on the correct side. One triangle.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      When two triangles fit, the solver solves and draws both, and you choose the one your problem needs. Most
      textbook errors happen here, so the solver does not choose for you.
    </p>

    <h2>Triangle area and its cross-check</h2>
    <p>
      When all three sides are known, Heron’s formula gives the area from the semi-perimeter
      s = (a + b + c) ÷ 2:
    </p>
    <p>Area = √( s(s − a)(s − b)(s − c) )</p>
    <p>
      The tool also computes ½·a·b·sin(C) and shows both, so you can check that they agree. The area gives the
      three heights (h = 2·area ÷ base), the inradius (area ÷ s) and the circumradius (abc ÷ 4·area).
    </p>

    <h2>Classifying the result</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>By sides: equilateral (all three equal), isosceles (exactly two equal), scalene (all different).</li>
      <li>By angles: right-angled (one angle exactly 90°), obtuse (one angle above 90°), acute (all below 90°).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A triangle can be both, for example isosceles and right-angled, like the 45-45-90 set square.
    </p>

    <h2>When no triangle exists</h2>
    <p>
      Two checks catch impossible inputs. The triangle inequality says each side must be shorter than the sum
      of the other two, so 2, 3, 9 cannot form a triangle. And the three angles must sum to exactly 180°, so
      120° and 70° leave nothing for the third. In both cases the solver explains the problem and shows no
      answer.
    </p>

    <h2>The diagram</h2>
    <p>
      The drawing comes from the solved coordinates, scaled to fit, so it matches your numbers: an almost flat
      triangle looks like a sliver, and an equilateral one looks equilateral. Each vertex shows its angle and
      each edge its length. The calculation runs in your browser.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
